WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.540
What happens when you keep a peeled banana

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.860
out in the open?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.680
The banana gradually deteriorates and we find

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.020
several tiny creatures hovering over it!

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.260
These tiny creatures are commonly called fruit flies.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.160
They’re annoying right?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.260
But did you know that these small wonders

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.320
are one of the most powerful tools for geneticists?

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.820
Sounds hard to believe!

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.000
But these fruit flies are used as model organisms

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.800
in Genetics.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.680
Let me tell you an extremely important and

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.200
unbelievable fact!

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.160
Did you know that the common fruit fly, Drosophila

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.760
melanogaster and humans have genes that are

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.740
almost 60% similar to each other?

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.340
You heard me right!

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.540
Just a total of eight chromosomes in this

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.420
tiny creature contain around 60% genes similar

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:00.840
to humans!

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.792
And not only this, but the fruit fly is also

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.480
used as a model to study the most serious

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.020
neurodegenerative disorders including Parkinson’s

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.400
and Alzheimer’s.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.780
So this little creature is nothing short of

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.220
a miracle for us!

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.340
But who thought of using these tiny model

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.160
organisms for experiments?

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.936
Well, the use of Drosophila melanogaster as

00:01:25.936 --> 00:01:29.790
model organisms dates back to several years!

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.559
However, the organism rose to fame with the

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:35.200
experiments of the father of experimental

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.020
genetics : Thomas Hunt Morgan!

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.820
Following Boveri and Sutton’s Chromosomal

00:01:41.826 --> 00:01:45.099
theory of inheritance, Morgan and his colleagues

00:01:45.099 --> 00:01:47.524
carried out many experiments on the common

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:50.180
fruit fly which served as further confirmation

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.370
for the theory!

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.992
Let us have a look at what experiments he

00:01:54.992 --> 00:01:58.880
actually performed, and what results he got!

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.537
For that, let us travel back in time and reach

00:02:02.537 --> 00:02:05.399
the early years of the 20th century!

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:08.310
It was around the early years of 1900s that

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.220
Thomas Hunt Morgan started working on fruit

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.110
flies with a determination to find something new!

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.060
Did he really find something interesting?

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.280
Well yes!

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.220
One fine day, in a vial containing red-eyed

00:02:22.223 --> 00:02:25.540
flies, Morgan found something strange!

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.580
He could spot a male fly with white coloured eyes!

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.820
Morgan knew that this is a special type of

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.780
mutant and experiments with this will lead

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.800
to a new path in Genetics!

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.830
So how did he plan his experiments?

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:41.820
What was the procedure?

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.760
Let us find that out in the next part!

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:51.700
Imagine you are working in a lab with the

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.980
little fruit fly!

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.660
One fine day, you find an unusual mutant among

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.540
the other wild types.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.420
Wild types means the ones normally found in

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.800
nature, or the natural forms to be precise!

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.780
So the ‘mutant form’ means the ones that

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.180
have undergone some random or unusual changes.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.198
For example, red eye colour is the natural

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.040
or wild type of variety in fruit flies.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.720
But the white eye colour is a mutant form.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.680
So what would you do if you find a mutant

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.000
type of fruit fly in a vial one day?

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:34.066
I am sure anyone would simply grab the opportunity

00:03:34.066 --> 00:03:38.040
and begin experimenting with this unique variety!

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.740
The father of experimental genetics, Thomas

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.500
Hunt Morgan was not an exception to this!

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.429
One fine day, he found a unique mutant in

00:03:47.429 --> 00:03:50.420
one of his vials while working in the lab.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.600
The mutant was a white eyed male fruit fly.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.054
Soon, he realised that the exclusive mutant

00:03:58.054 --> 00:04:02.150
was an ideal model for carrying several experiments

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:05.420
that would lead us to new paths in genetics!

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.793
Let us begin understanding how Morgan carried

00:04:08.793 --> 00:04:12.140
out his famous experiments on the fruit fly

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.500
which later proved the Chromosomal theory

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.460
of inheritance better!

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.360
Morgan crossed the white eyed male with a

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.750
wild type, that is a red eyed female.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.560
The F1 generation had all the offsprings with

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.340
red coloured eyes.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.480
This showed that red eye colour is a dominant trait!

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.160
Okay!

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.160
What next then?

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.260
Morgan stepped ahead to cross the F1 hybrids.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.960
What he expected was a 3 to 1 ratio.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.340
That is what we get in the F2 generation, right?

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.910
And what should the genotypic ratio be?

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.200
It should be 1 to 2 to 1.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.500
And is this what Morgan obtained?

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.740
Yes!

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.960
Morgan obtained 3 red eyed flies and one white

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.920
eyed fly in the F2 generation.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:09.540
Also, the genotypic ratio was 1 to 2 to 1.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.220
So did these results suggest that Mendel’s

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.620
experiments were correct?

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.880
Kind of Yes!

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.320
Mendel had obtained ratios for plants.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:25.000
Here, we considered animals for the experiment.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.740
That is why, although Morgan had obtained

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.820
the ratios just like Mendel did, things didn’t

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:31.980
end here!

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.440
Morgan obtained only one white eyed fly in

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:36.900
the F2 generation.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:39.820
But note that this fly is a MALE.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.940
This unfolds a lot of hidden ambiguities.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.140
Why didn’t he obtain a white eyed FEMALE?

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.360
Did this hint at something?

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.320
That’s a right guess!

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.080
The results not only gave a proof of the Chromosomal

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.500
theory of Inheritance, but also helped in

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.300
shining light on newer concepts.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:06.300
Morgan assumed that the eye colour genes that

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.220
are passed down the generation are sex linked.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:12.020
Now what could this be?

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.700
Let us get introduced to this term first.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.360
We know that chromosomes in organisms are

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.620
of two types.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.440
One type is called autosomes, which are simply,

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:26.300
not the sex chromosomes.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.860
And the second category is this sex chromosomes

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.100
themselves which determine the sex of the

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.660
organism.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.080
These chromosomes are usually represented

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.240
with the letters “X” and “Y”.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:43.094
In most organisms, individuals with “X”

00:06:43.094 --> 00:06:46.620
chromosomes are females, while individuals

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.980
with one “X” and one “Y” are males.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.960
Getting back to our concept, sex linked implies

00:06:53.965 --> 00:06:56.570
the characters or traits linked to the sex

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:57.880
chromosomes.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.840
So the characters which usually pass down

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.330
the generations with respect to a particular

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.870
sex will be sex linked.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.517
So in this case, Morgan did not stop at the

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.100
fact that his results confirmed the chromosomal

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:13.000
theory.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.000
He went a step ahead and put forth the assumption

00:07:18.006 --> 00:07:20.680
that the genes for eye colour are present

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:23.800
on the “X” chromosome in the fruit fly.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.760
In the next part, let us understand what Morgan

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:30.440
had to say about this!

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.880
We are in the midst of studying Thomas Morgan’s

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:35.940
experiment.

00:07:35.949 --> 00:07:38.689
We have seen so far that Morgan crossed a

00:07:38.689 --> 00:07:41.630
white eyed male with a red eyed female which

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.340
is the wild type.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:47.620
The F1 generation had all the offsprings with

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:48.800
red coloured eyes.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.480
Also, when he obtained the F2 generation,

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.180
it made him assume that the genes for eye

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:57.872
colour are present on the “X” chromosome

00:07:57.880 --> 00:07:59.150
in the fruit flies.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.560
Now to understand this better, let us carry

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.700
out a theoretical cross.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:08.220
To begin with, let us denote the chromosomes

00:08:08.221 --> 00:08:10.539
as “X” and “Y” for representation

00:08:10.539 --> 00:08:12.110
purposes.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:14.847
But if we use “X” and “Y” to represent

00:08:14.847 --> 00:08:17.800
the chromosomes, then how do we represent

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:19.979
the genes on these chromosomes?

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.760
For that, we will write additional letters

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:23.960
in superscript!

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.220
So let’s say we write the letter “w”

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.620
in lower case to represent the genes for white

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.360
coloured eyes.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.496
The red eyed phenotype will then be represented

00:08:34.496 --> 00:08:37.099
by the letter “w” in lower case, having

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:38.990
a plus sign after it.

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:43.139
Do you think this representation is strange?

00:08:43.139 --> 00:08:47.410
Well, that’s how it is usually done in genetics.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:50.098
The first letter of the mutant type is used

00:08:50.098 --> 00:08:51.660
for denoting the alleles.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.357
Hence, we use the letter “w” for representing

00:08:55.357 --> 00:08:58.069
the mutant allele for white eyes.

00:08:58.069 --> 00:09:01.200
We simply add the plus sign to it to denote

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.360
the wild type.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.480
So instead of using the letter “r” for

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.960
red eye colour, we use “w plus”.

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:11.160
Also, we need to know that the wild type which

00:09:11.167 --> 00:09:13.839
is the red eye trait is DOMINANT over the

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:16.940
mutant type which is the white eye trait.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:20.879
Getting back to the experiment, Morgan assumed

00:09:20.879 --> 00:09:23.420
that the genes for the trait are present on

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:24.560
“X” chromosome.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:28.620
Let us also ASSUME the same and begin crossing

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.120
and see if we get the same results!

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:34.300
So we have X with ‘w in lower case’ for

00:09:34.309 --> 00:09:37.430
representing the genes that give white coloured

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:40.446
eyes, and we have “X with w plus” for

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:43.869
representing the wild type that is the red

00:09:43.869 --> 00:09:45.540
coloured eyes.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.532
Now we begin the crossing of the white eyed

00:09:48.532 --> 00:09:50.620
male with the red eyed female.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:53.740
The cross yields all the offsprings with red

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:56.200
eyes in the F1 generation.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:59.880
This is how the genes are passed to the progeny.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.260
Now the next step is crossing the offsprings

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:05.340
from this F1 generation.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.560
On crossing these two with red eyes, we get

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.240
a progeny that is in the ratio 3 to 1.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.140
So we have got three that are red eyed and

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:17.380
one that is white eyed.

00:10:17.589 --> 00:10:21.120
To be precise, we obtain one male with red

00:10:21.126 --> 00:10:24.579
eyes, and two females which are red eyed.

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:28.100
The only white eyed fly is this male here.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.940
Morgan obtained this same result if you remember!

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.580
So this assumption we made gave us the same

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:37.620
results as Morgan’s cross.

00:10:38.189 --> 00:10:40.680
Hence the assumption that the genes for eye

00:10:40.696 --> 00:10:43.029
colour are present on the “X” chromosome

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:45.300
in fruit flies is correct!

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.460
What if the alleles would have been present

00:10:48.466 --> 00:10:49.699
on “Y” chromosome?

00:10:49.699 --> 00:10:54.160
Then the trait would have been visible ONLY in males.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:57.842
That is because in organisms like Drosophila,

00:10:57.842 --> 00:11:01.769
males have one X chromosome and one Y chromosome

00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:04.640
and females have both ‘X’ chromosomes.

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:08.580
To summarise, this experiment helped Morgan

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.620
to prove that genes are present on chromosomes.

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:14.760
Also, he could find out that the genes for

00:11:14.779 --> 00:11:17.639
eye colour were present on the "X" chromosome

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:18.920
in the fruit fly.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.240
This also indicated the fact that genes are

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:25.020
passed from one generation to the other!

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.480
This is how Mendelian genetics as well as

00:11:28.495 --> 00:11:31.019
Sutton and Boveri’s chromosomal theory of

00:11:31.019 --> 00:11:34.459
inheritance were both proved better with Morgan’s

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:36.960
experiments!

